body:
Reproduced with plain PHP:

``` php
<?php

class Related
{
    public $owner;
}

class Model
{
    public $related;
    public $type = 0;

    public function __construct($type)
    {
        $this->related = new Related();
        $this->related->owner = $this;

        $this->type = $type;
    }
}

$models = array(
    0 => new Model(1),
    1 => new Model(1),
);

$args = [
    [0 => 1, 1 => 1],
    SORT_ASC,
    SORT_REGULAR,
];
$args[] = &$models;

var_dump(call_user_func_array('array_multisort', $args));
print_r($models);
```

As far as I see the error results from the fact that the given ordering does not result in an ordering so PHP will compare the objects directly and this results in the error:

``` php
var_dump($models[0] < $models[1]);
```

Seems that the fix is to force a default ordering if given columns do not specify an ordering:
https://github.com/yiisoft/yii/issues/2082#issuecomment-38397101
need to verify this.
